e Hotel"],"y":1862,"f":"exact","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"1862","h":8,"osm":"relation/5313065","kind":"residential","listEntry":1265261,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1265261","qid":"Q26555868","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Stable%20Court%2C%20Bestwood%20Lodge.jpg","sum":"Bestwood Lodge Stables is a residential building on Bestwood Lodge Drive. The building was built in 1862, according to Historic England (via Wikipedia). It is Grade II listed as Stable Court at Bestwood Lodge Hotel.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Gedling Borough Council","xy":[-1.15253,53.01364]},"179199":{"h":10,"osm":"way/355827297","council":"Gedling Borough Council","xy":[-1.15231,53.01319]},"179200":{"y":1862,"f":"exact","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"1862","h":10,"osm":"way/355827314","listEntry":1227445,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1227445","qid":"Q26521352","sum":"The building was built in 1862, according to Historic England (via Wikipedia). It is Grade II listed.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Gedling Borough Council","xy":[-1.15265,53.01329]},"179201":{"today":"Bestwood Lodge Hotel","y":1864,"f":"approx","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"1862–65","h":10,"osm":"way/355827274","kind":"hotel","listEntry":1227444,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1227444","qid":"Q17547480","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bestwood%20Lodge%20Hotel%20-%20geograph.org.uk%20-%201780330.jpg","archive":[{"file":"CS p3.134 - Bestwood Lodge, Nottinghamshire - Morris's County Seats, 1879.jpg","year":1866,"author":null,"licence":"Public domain"}],"article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bestwood_Lodge","cat":"Bestwood Lodge","stories":[{"layer":"bestwood","text":"The first written record of a royal hunting lodge at Bestwood is from 1286, in the reign of Edward I. Charles II and Nell Gwyn visited it, and in 1682 he leased the lodge to her. Their son, Charles Beauclerk, Earl of Burford, was made Duke of St Albans in 1684, and Bestwood passed down through his descendants.","source":{"title":"Bestwood Lodge","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bestwood_Lodge","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1286","whenSrc":{"title":"Bestwood Lodge","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bestwood_Lodge","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"A royal hunting park"},{"layer":"bestwood","text":"The present lodge was built in 1862–65 for the 10th Duke of St Albans by Samuel Sanders Teulon, in the Gothic Revival style. The army took it over in the Second World War, and in the mid-1970s it became a hotel. It is listed Grade II*.","source":{"title":"Bestwood Lodge","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bestwood_Lodge","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1862","whenSrc":{"title":"Bestwood Lodge","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bestwood_Lodge","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Bestwood Lodge"}],"sum":"Bestwood Lodge is a Grade II* listed 19th-century country house in the civil parish of Deer Park, Nottinghamshire, England. It was built in the Gothic Revival style in 1862–65 for the 10th Duke of St Albans by architect Samuel Sanders Teulon. It has been operated as the Bestwood Lodge Hotel since the 1970s.","sumSrc":{"title":"Bestwood Lodge","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bestwood_Lodge"},"council":"Gedling Borough Council","xy":[-1.15257,53.01287]},"179202":{"where":"Chiltern Close","h":8,"osm":"way/1110424784","kind":"detached","council":"Gedling Borough Council","xy":[-1.15507,53.0137]},"179203":{"where":"Chiltern Close","h":10,"osm":"way/1110424785","council":"Gedling Borough Council","xy":[-1.15507,53.01376]},"179204":{"where":"Chiltern Close","h":8,"osm":"way/1110424786","kind":"detached","council":"Gedling Borough Council","xy":[-1.15507,53.0138]},"17